   `[driver].merge_requires = "required"` opts back into host-config-only
   semantics, and skips the behind check and the update too.
 
+  A brief's `Merges with: <ids>` names an *atomic merge group*: bundles that must
+  not land on the base separately (either side declaring it binds both; a group is
+  every bundle connected that way). The driver reads the field off every bundle on
+  disk, not only the run's own, archived ones in `completed/` too (where a bundle is
+  in both places, the active one counts). So a declaration still binds its partner
+  when the bundle that made it is held, never scheduled, finished by an earlier run,
+  or archived since.
+
+  At a wave boundary the driver does not ready or merge a COMPLETE member while its
+  group cannot all merge: a member is not COMPLETE, or a member has a PR merge mode
+  will not merge. That is a publish record merge mode refuses (no recorded PR because
+  its publish failed, say), or, for a member an earlier run finished, a PR merge mode
+  could not have opened: one against another bundle's branch, or a stacked PR whose
+  branch carries other bundles' work (a stack-mode run's, say). Such a member counts
+  as merged only once its PR has merged into its own target. The driver checks every
+  member before it merges the first, so a refusal the run can see coming never lands
+  the group in part. A held member's PR stays a draft, one line says what it
+  waits for, and the run goes on. A bundle that depends on a held member, or that
+  `Conflicts with` one and is built after it, is not built this run, and is named
+  with the group. At the boundary where nothing blocks the group any more, it merges
+  back to back, each member through the same gates as any other PR: members an
+  earlier run finished first, then by wave, then by name. A member an earlier run
+  finished is still part of its group, so a re-run completes a group the first run
+  had to hold: drive the members still to build — by `pdca flow <ids>`, naming the
+  finished ones or not, or by the sweep — and the first boundary that can merge the
+  group merges the finished member with the rest. A member that ends DISCONTINUED,
+  or is not built, leaves the rest unmerged. The final wave is the exception, as for
+  every PR: its PRs stay drafts, so a group whose last member finishes there, or
+  that sits wholly in it, is not merged by the run. At its end the run names each
+  group it did not merge whole, with why and what to do; for a group nothing blocks,
+  that is "merge its PRs together", in the order given. Exit 3 marks a PR that a
+  boundary would have merged and held instead: a run that held a member at a
+  boundary exits 3 when every bundle finished, even if the group's last member then
+  finished in the final wave. A group wholly in the final wave holds nothing, since
+  every final-wave PR stays a draft, group or not, so it does not change the exit
+  code. GitHub cannot merge two PRs at
+  once: if the host refuses a member's merge after another member merged, the run
+  stops as for any refused merge and names which members landed.
+
+  Some group shapes cannot work in merge mode, and `pdca flow <ids>` refuses them
+  before anything builds (exit 2, with the ways out). A member the run cannot
+  complete: one that is not among the run's bundles and not COMPLETE (in flight but
+  not named, never briefed, or DISCONTINUED); name it in the same run, or remove the
+  `Merges with` edge to one that ended. Members that `Conflicts with` each other: the
+  later one would be built on a base without the held earlier one, so landing it
+  would need an update across the declared overlap, which can stop the run with the
+  group half landed. A group that waits for itself: a member that depends on another
+  member, directly, through other bundles, or through a bundle that `Conflicts with`
+  a member and is built after it, could only build after the group merged, which the
+  group forbids; and two groups, each with a member that depends on the other group,
+  wait for each other the same way. Which side of a `Conflicts with` pair is built
+  first is read off the order the waves are really built in, which is not always
+  name order: ordering one pair can put a path between the two sides of another.
+  Make such bundles one bundle, or run the batch
+  in `wave_mode = "stack"`, where the field changes nothing: you merge the stack and
+  can take the group together. A `pdca flow --from-csv` sweep holds such a group
+  instead, and a group one of whose members it holds for any other reason, then what
+  depends on them, and drives the rest. A split during the run re-levels only what
+  is left to build, but a group member the run has already built still counts as one
+  of its bundles there.
+
 In merge mode the merge is unattended, so **publish refuses to open a PR against
 any branch this run produced** — the work must land on a base that exists
 independently of the run, or the run stops and says so. Two shapes get refused,
